<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Troyes</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Augustobona, Trecae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Troyes is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> in the Champagne region of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Founded under Augustus as Augustobona, the chief town of the Tricasses, it later took their name. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the capital of the counts of Champagne and the seat of two of the great Champagne fairs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chretien-de-troyes">Chrétien de Troyes</a> wrote his Arthurian romances at their court. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-troyes">Treaty of Troyes</a> was signed there in 1420.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/troyes.jpg" alt="Troyes" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KBWEi at German Wikipedia (Original text: KBWEi 21:50, 14. Okt. 2007 (CEST)) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Troyes_centre_ville1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rashi of <b>Troyes</b>, in the 11th century, wrote the most widely used commentary on the Bible and Talmud; Moses Maimonides, in 12th-century Cairo, wrote the Mishneh Torah, a code of all Jewish law, and the Guide for the Perplexed, a work of philosophy; Joseph Karo’s Shulchan Aruch of…</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Kept to few occupations, many lent money at interest, which the Church forbade Christians to do; others traded, and the commentary of Rashi of <b>Troyes</b> (1040–1105) is printed in every edition of the Talmud.</blockquote><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Social Hierarchy of Feudal Europe</a></li><li><blockquote>A church council at <b>Troyes</b>, led by a papal legate and inspired by Bernard of Clairvaux, approved a rule for the Knights Templar.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-13">13 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His grandson Odo II added <b>Troyes</b> and Meaux around 1022, encircling the Capetian royal domain and contending with Anjou for Touraine, which was lost in 1044.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-blois">County of Blois</a></li><li><blockquote>The counties of <b>Troyes</b> and Meaux were built up in the 10th century by the counts of Vermandois, descendants of Herbert II, and passed around 1022 to Odo II of Blois.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-champagne">County of Champagne</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Rome they formed a civitas with <b>Augustobona</b> as its chief town, later called Trecae after the people, the origin of Troyes.</blockquote><a href="/state/tricasses">Tricasses</a></li></ul></section>
